//? Title: Estimate two changes of slope in a continuous curve
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Fit a continuous line with two breakpoints

The signal first rises, then falls, and then rises again. The example
fits three straight segments connected at two unknown breakpoints.
At each candidate breakpoint, the solver refits the slopes. An
estimated breakpoint can fall between measurements.

Each profile interval fixes one breakpoint and refits the other
breakpoint and all the line coefficients. The intervals are pointwise
and use asymptotic likelihood-ratio calibration.

```{cpp, id=fit-breakpoints, file="figures/segmented_regression.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/segmented_regression.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    std::vector<double> position, signal;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 30; ++i) {
        position.push_back(i);
        signal.push_back(2 + .5 * i - 1.2 * std::max(0., i - 8.3)
                         + 1.6 * std::max(0., i - 21.6) + .18 * std::sin(i * 2.4));
    }
    const auto fit = stats::segmented_regression(position, signal, {
        .breakpoints = 2, .minimum_segment_size = 3,
    });
    if (!fit.converged || fit.intervals.size() != fit.breakpoints.size())
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected a converged fit with both intervals");
    std::println("| Change | Breakpoint | 95% profile interval | Slope before | Slope after |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < fit.breakpoints.size(); ++j) {
        const auto& interval = fit.intervals[j];
        if (interval.lower.status != stats::ProfileEndpointStatus::Crossing ||
            interval.upper.status != stats::ProfileEndpointStatus::Crossing)
            throw std::runtime_error("Expected two likelihood-ratio crossings");
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                     j + 1, fit.breakpoints[j], interval.lower.value, interval.upper.value,
                     fit.slopes[j], fit.slopes[j + 1]);
    }

    std::vector<double> vertices{fit.origin, fit.breakpoints[0], fit.breakpoints[1], position.back()};
    std::vector<double> heights{fit.value_at_origin};
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < fit.slopes.size(); ++j)
        heights.push_back(heights.back() + fit.slopes[j] * (vertices[j + 1] - vertices[j]));
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create()
        .line(vertices, heights, "Fitted segments", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .points(position, signal, "Observations", {51, 65, 85}, 5)
        .abline_v(fit.breakpoints[0], {100, 116, 139}, 1)
        .abline_v(fit.breakpoints[1], {100, 116, 139}, 1)
        .title("Continuous segmented regression").x_label("Position").y_label("Signal")
        .size(740, 420).save_svg("build/segmented_regression.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Change | Breakpoint | 95% profile interval | Slope before | Slope after |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 8.308 | [8.129, 8.491] | 0.498 | -0.701 |
| 2 | 21.595 | [21.458, 21.731] | -0.701 | 0.902 |
\image(figures/segmented_regression.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The three fitted segments meet at the estimated breakpoints.)
\alttext(A blue line rises to the first breakpoint near 8.3, descends to the second near 21.6, and then rises. Points lie close to each segment.)

The joint search uses several deterministic starting configurations.
Each step searches one breakpoint over its feasible range, so the
reported convergence describes the coordinate search for the best fit found.

[Hudson (1966)|https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1966.10482198] ·
[Muggeo (2003)|https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.1545]
